# Example 23: Digital evolution - organisms evolve a signal task (S3)
# A population of digital organisms (small instruction genomes) is
# evolved by mutation and selection on a signal-production task, the
# digital-organism paradigm of Avida (Ofria & Wilke 2004, Artificial
# Life 10:191) and Lenski et al. 2003 (Nature 423:139).  Fitness is an
# exponential of the number of target bits reproduced, so every extra
# correct bit confers a constant selective advantage - the digital analog
# of the small-effect beneficial mutations of the E. coli LTEE (Lenski
# 2017, Phil Trans R Soc B 372:20160592).
#
# The digital organism must learn to emit a 12-bit target signal:
#   1010 1100 1011
# The evolutionary dynamics are driven by the Python API shown at the
# end; the HelixLang program below keeps this file in the example suite
# (a signal gene emitting the standard hello program).
#
#gene name=signal
ATG GCT GGT GTA TAA
#end
#
#config ticks=300 output=stdout
#
# # Python API for the digital evolution run:
# #
# # from helixlang.apps.digital_evolution import (DigitalEvolution,
# #                                              DigitalEvolutionConfig)
# # evo = DigitalEvolution(DigitalEvolutionConfig(
# #     population_size=200,
# #     genome_length=12,
# #     target=(1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1),
# #     substitution_rate=0.01,      # per-instruction mutation rate
# #     insertion_rate=0.001,
# #     deletion_rate=0.001,
# #     generations=300,
# #     seed=3,
# # ))
# # evo.run()
